@lemon0333 I'd like to see this land, so I rebased it on today's main to check where it stands: it applies cleanly and `tests/catalog/test_glue.py` + `test_dynamodb.py` pass (98 tests, including your 7 role tests), ruff is clean.

One thing I'd raise before a maintainer review: `_get_aws_session_with_assumed_role` builds the session from the static credentials of a single `AssumeRole` call, so `session.get_credentials()` is a plain `Credentials` with no refresh. A long-lived process (a service holding a `GlueCatalog`) starts getting `ExpiredToken` once the role session ends, one hour by default. The S3 FileIO does not have this problem as far as I can tell, since it hands the ARN to Arrow, which refreshes through the SDK's STS provider. botocore can do the same here with `AssumeRoleCredentialFetcher` + `DeferredRefreshableCredentials`.

Do you want to reopen this and carry on? If you don't have time for it anymore, I'm happy to open a new PR from your commit with the refresh added, keeping you as co-author.

This is ready for review. CI is green, and the integration tests run against `apache/iceberg-rest-fixture`.

There are three open questions under "Not in this PR" in the description, and I'd welcome a maintainer's view on them before polishing further: retry on conflict, creating tables in the same commit, and the `Catalog.commit_transactions(*transactions)` entry point.

@kevinjqliu @Fokko, would one of you have time to take a look?

Hi everyone, thank you all for the thoughtful discussion here! (´｡• ᵕ •｡`)

I've opened #4201 based on the approach you outlined: the expiration is committed first, and only then are the files referenced solely by the expired snapshots deleted. Since deletion happens after the commit, the table isn't held up while files are being removed. It's opt-in via `clean_expired_files()`, and CI is all green. ʕ•ᴥ•ʔ

@Fokko @Anton-Tarazi @ForeverAngry since this direction came from you, I'd be really grateful if you could take a look whenever you have a moment. No rush at all, and any feedback is very welcome! (๑•̀ㅂ•́)و✧

@RyanLin5967 thanks for catching this one — the failure is silent, so the branch looks fine until you actually read it (๑•̀ㅂ•́)و✧

The fix is small: +102 / −7 across four files, and most of that is tests. Five places that look up a branch head by name now fall back to main's current snapshot when the branch does not exist, matching Java. That covers append, overwrite, delete, and writes that get retried after a branch is removed concurrently. Reverting any one of the five makes a test fail.

One check is red and not from this PR: `Run zizmor` flags `pypa/[email removed]` in `nightly-pypi-build.yml`, which this PR does not touch. #4216 bumps it to v1.14.3, so this should go green once that lands.

@Fokko ready for review when you have a moment (´｡• ᵕ •｡`)

@RyanLin5967 thanks for flagging this one — a `count()` of 0 looks like an answer, so nothing points you at a snapshot that is simply gone (๑•̀ㅂ•́)و✧

The fix is tiny: two lines in `_plan_files_local` plus one test, +13 / −0 across two files. `plan_files()` and `count()` now raise `Snapshot not found` when the scan names an id that is not in the metadata, which is what `to_arrow()` already does. An empty table still plans to nothing, and the test pins id `0` so the check cannot be fooled by a falsy id.

One check is red and not from this PR: `Run zizmor` flags `pypa/[email removed]` in `nightly-pypi-build.yml`, a file this change does not touch. #4216 bumps it to v1.14.3, so this should go green once that lands.

@Fokko ready for review when you have a moment (´｡• ᵕ •｡`)
